{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Data Types", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 8, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 2, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use when talking about data types.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations. Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.   Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob08re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple Present, Present Continuous and Be Going to in Negative Sentences and Questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<p>The <strong>simple present</strong> tense, the <strong>present continuous</strong> tense and the future tense with <strong>be going</strong> can all be used to express negative statements and ask questions in different contexts.</p>", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Do you know anything about data types?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I don't understand that topic, and I am taking an exam next Monday.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "My explanation is not going to be enough. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I am not doing anything right now.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I am not going out this weekend.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<p>We normally use the <strong>present</strong> <strong>simple</strong> tense when we want to talk about routines or facts. In the negative form, the present simple has the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + Do/Does + Not + Infinitive verb + Complement</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>She <strong>doesn&rsquo;t work</strong> on C++.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>When asking questions, the simple present has the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Do/Does + Subject + Infinitive verb + Complement</p> <p>&nbsp;For example:</p> <p><strong><i>Do</i></strong><i> you <strong>know</strong> how to recompile a JAVA code?</i></p> <p><i>&nbsp;</i></p> <p>On the other hand, we use the <strong>present</strong> <strong>continuous</strong> to describe activities happening right now or in a planned future, depending on the context and time complement. In the negative form, the <strong>present</strong> <strong>continuous</strong> has the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Subject + Verb to be + Not + Verb-ing + Complement</p> <p>&nbsp;For example:</p> <p><i>We <strong>aren&rsquo;t writing</strong> any apps in C++ now.</i></p> <p><i>He i<strong>sn&rsquo;t</strong> <strong>working</strong> on that database for the next month. </i></p> <p><i>&nbsp;</i></p> <p>When asking questions, the <strong>present</strong> <strong>continuous</strong> has the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Verb to be + Subject + Verb-ing + Complement</p> <p>For example:</p> <p>&nbsp;<strong><i>Are</i></strong><i> you <strong>determining</strong> the kind of values?</i></p> <p><strong><i>Are</i></strong><i> they <strong>compiling </strong>the floating point numbers<strong>?</strong></i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Finally, we use the future form <strong>be going to</strong> when we describe the intention to do something in the future. In the negative form, the future form <strong>be going to</strong> has the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + Verb to be + Not + Going to + Infinitive verb + Complement</p> <p>&nbsp;For example:</p> <p><i>They <strong>aren&rsquo;t going to </strong>include new features in this new version.</i></p> <p><i>&nbsp;</i></p> <p>When asking questions, the future form <strong>be going to</strong> has the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Verb to be + Subject + Going to + Infinitive verb + Complement</p> <p><strong>&nbsp;</strong>For example:</p> <p><strong><i>Is</i></strong><i> he <strong>going to </strong>include the missing characters? </i></p>", "type": "text"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Modal Verbs Must and Have to in Affirmative Sentences", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "The modal verbs <strong>have to</strong> and <strong>must</strong> are used when we talk about obligations.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "I have to pass this exam or I am failing the course.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I have to apply myself if I want to graduate. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You must study hard this weekend.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You must remember that data types depend on the programming language that you are using.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<p>The modals <strong>have to</strong> and <strong>must</strong> express a strong obligation. However, each one has a slightly different application.</p> <p>We use the modal <strong>have to</strong> in order to describe an obligation that does not depend on the speaker. In other words, the circumstances that make the situation obligatory are external to the person talking. The following are examples of the modal have to in affirmative sentences:</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p><i>Johan <strong>has to</strong> finish this code by 8 am.</i></p> <p><i>They <strong>have to</strong> organize this database.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>On the other hand, we use the modal <strong>must</strong> when talking about an obligation that relates to the personal circumstances of the person speaking. In other words, the speaker agrees with the obligation, which is in consonance with their beliefs. The following are examples of the modal must in affirmative sentences:</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p><i>I <strong>must</strong> hurry if I want to finish this code.</i></p> <p><i>You <strong>must</strong> be organized when trying to recompile the whole code.</i></p>", "type": "text"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "enough ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "sufficient or adequate", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: My explanation is not going to be enough. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob08aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "state ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to establish or determine", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It also states how the computer processes and stores these values.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob08aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "off the top of one's head", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to remember without thinking much", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I can mention a few off the top of my head. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob08aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "fractional ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "division or part of a complete unit", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Then, we have floating point numbers or floats, which are numbers that can contain a fractional part.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob08aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "alphanumeric ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "composed of numbers and letters", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Finally, there are strings, which can be a combination of alphanumeric symbols. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob08aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "to apply oneself ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to work or study hard", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I have to apply myself if I want to graduate.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob08aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Look for more information about Data types. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Create a post with the main characteristics of Data types.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share with your partners the post you created.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I look for more information about Data types.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I create a post with the main characteristics of Data types.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share with your partners the post you created.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can explain different data types.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can explain how data is classified in different types.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can describe different types of data.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "How does a computer differentiate a number from a letter?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Why is it important to separate different classes of data? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "gro